---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
    title="Family Pass v1.0 · Sanctum Haus"
    description="What's in Sanctum Family Pass v1.0, what's coming in v1.1, why Firewalla, and why a one-time $129 license."
>
    <main>
        <p class="tagline">Family Pass · v1.0</p>

        <h1>Family Pass, v1.0</h1>

        <p class="lede">
            A one-time license for the haus you actually own. Lifetime.
            Yours forever. No subscription that can disappear next
            quarter, no "your data may be used to improve our models," no
            cloud round-trip in the hot path.
        </p>

        <h2>What's in v1.0</h2>

        <h3>Sanctum Core</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>The council</strong> — Yoda, Qui-Gon, Windu,
                Cilghal, and friends. Eight specialized agents,
                orchestrated through a single voice agent on your phone.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The cathedral</strong> — Qwen 3.6 35B-A3B-4bit
                running locally on Apple Silicon at 50+ tokens/sec. No
                OpenAI bill, no rate limits, no terms-of-service drift.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>OpenClaw VM</strong> — Lima-managed Ubuntu VM
                with the agent runtime, isolated from your Mac host.
                Snapshots, restorable, dependency-free on the macOS
                side.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Force Flow</strong> — your haus's nervous
                system. Routes alerts, watches the watchers, fails
                loud, runs on loopback so it can't get pwned from your
                LAN.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Backup + Watchdogs</strong> — daily encrypted
                backups, self-healing LaunchAgents, hands-off recovery
                from the kinds of small breakages macOS quietly hands
                you on Sunday morning.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>mTLS everywhere</strong> — every internal
                service connection mutually authenticated. Defense in
                depth, not just at the LAN edge.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Screen Time module</h3>
        <p>
            The parental-controls system that actually works when Apple's
            don't. Sanctum Screen Time enforces curfews, wind-down
            windows, homework gates, and per-device credits at the
            <em>network</em> level — through Firewalla MAC-based blocks
            — so it survives factory resets, fresh Apple IDs, screen
            recording, and the rest of the workarounds that make Apple
            Family Sharing a coin flip.
        </p>
        <p>
            Ships with a PWA on <code>localhost:5151</code>, a Home
            Assistant integration, and a Holocron panel. Schedule lives
            in YAML, version-controllable, diff-friendly.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <h2>What's coming in v1.1</h2>
        <p class="muted">
            None of this is in the v1.0 pilot. Listed so you know what
            you're aiming at.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Stripe checkout</strong> — the actual
                $129-one-time-license flow. v1.0 pilot is free and
                inviation-based; v1.1 opens the door.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>PWA polish</strong> — touch targets, offline
                bundle, the parts of mobile UX that are obvious in
                retrospect.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Cilghal as family assistant</strong> — the
                council's calmest member, exposed as a per-person AI
                you talk to instead of typing into.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Eero adapter</strong> — Firewalla is the
                tier-1 router story; Eero is the next one. Adapter
                interface designed so we don't have to fork v2 every
                time a new mesh-router brand wants in.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Cloud Sync</strong> — optional, $5/mo,
                customer-owned R2 or B2 bucket. Cross-device memory
                sync for the Holocron mobile app. If you don't want
                it, don't enable it — the local core works without it
                forever.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Yoda voice</strong> — the LiveKit-based phone
                agent, packaged for non-developer install. v1.0 ships
                with the council you type at; v1.1 ships with the
                council you call.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Cathedral local LLM autoswap</strong> — when
                Qwen 3.7 or whatever comes next is the right model,
                you get it without a manual surgery.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <hr />

        <h2>Why Firewalla</h2>
        <p>
            We could have built our own MAC-blocking firewall.
            Re-implementing what Firewalla does well — DPI, route-level
            blocks, the whole evasion-resistant enforcement story — is a
            multi-year project. Firewalla already ships hardware that does
            this, on a router-grade chassis you keep behind your
            internet-facing modem. They take care of the part that has to
            be brutal and we take care of the part that has to be
            thoughtful.
        </p>
        <p>
            The architecturally important property: Sanctum's screen-time
            rules are
            <em>installed into the Firewalla itself</em>. If the Sanctum
            bridge process crashes overnight, the Firewalla keeps
            enforcing whatever was last set. The kids do not get an
            uncontrolled internet window when our software goes sideways.
            This is what we mean by fail-closed — the dangerous failure
            mode is the absent one, not the dropping-blocks one.
        </p>
        <p>
            We're an adapter on top of Firewalla, not a replacement for
            it. You buy a Firewalla anyway. Sanctum makes it smarter, not
            optional.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <h2>Why a one-time $129 license</h2>
        <p>
            I can't keep maintaining this for free. I also refuse to make
            it depend on the cloud, which means I refuse to fund it through
            recurring API costs that I push onto you. A one-time payment
            for the premium features pays for the maintenance time. The
            open-source core stays free forever.
        </p>
        <p>
            $129 is the price of one month of a mid-tier cloud AI
            subscription. You pay it once. We do not have a renewal team,
            a churn dashboard, or a quarterly board meeting where someone
            asks how to raise the floor.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
            v1.0 pilot is free for invited haushelds — see <a
                href="/pilot/">the pilot page</a> if you want in.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <h2>Ready to install?</h2>
        <p>
            See <a href="/install/">the short install guide</a> here on the
            haus site, or the full
            <a href="https://www.sanctum.run/getting-started/installation/"
                >installation runbook</a
            >
            on the docs site. The pilot installer is hash-pinned and
            two-stepped — you check the SHA-256, you read the script, and
            then you run it. Trust is earned, not curled.
        </p>
    </main>
</Base>
